.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt SHRINK-PDF 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm shrink-pdf
.Nd make a PDF smaller by recompressing it with Ghostscript
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Ar infile
.Op Ar outfile | Fl
.Op Ar dpi
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
rewrites
.Ar infile
with Ghostscript, its images downsampled to
.Ar dpi ,
72 by default, and its fonts embedded and subset.
The result goes to
.Ar outfile ,
or to the standard output when it is
.Ql -
or left out.
.Pp
The recompression is lossy, and not always smaller: when the result is
no smaller than the input, the input itself is written instead.
Ghostscript writes to a temporary file first, so a failed run leaves
.Ar outfile
as it was.
An
.Ar outfile
that exists keeps its mode and its links; a new one gets the mode the
umask gives.
.Pp
Writing the result over the input, under its own name or through a
link, is refused: Ghostscript would read the file being truncated.
An
.Ar infile
whose name starts with
.Ql -
is read as a file, not as an option.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl v
Report the sizes before and after, on stderr, and whether the input
was kept.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TMPDIR
.It Ev TMPDIR
Where the temporary file is made, instead of
.Pa /tmp .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The result, or the input, was written.
.It 1
.Ar infile
cannot be read, Ghostscript is missing, or it failed.
.It 2
A usage error:
.Ar dpi
is not a positive number, or
.Ar outfile
is
.Ar infile .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Shrink a scan to send by mail:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ shrink-pdf -v scan.pdf scan-small.pdf
.Ed
.Pp
Keep the images sharper, at 150 dpi:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ shrink-pdf report.pdf report-small.pdf 150
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gs 1
.Sh HISTORY
After Alfred Klomp's
.Pa shrinkpdf.sh
.Pq BSD 3-Clause ,
.Lk http://alfredklomp.com/programming/shrinkpdf/ .
